{
	title: "A tab's own change costs the host one row, not all tabs",
	spec: "A turn's host cost does not grow with open tabs times clients. When one tab's state, attention or question changes (state, turn-start, prompt, turn-end events), tabs.update(id) builds only that row; if it equals the row clients last heard (tabs.state.rows) nothing is sent, otherwise the host saves tabs and sends {type: 'tab', tab} to every client. Structural or meta changes (open, close, move, rename, color, cwd) and every connect still send the full 'tabs' list, which resets the remembered rows. The client connection core keeps the last 'tabs' list, replaces the row named by a 'tab' event and emits the result as an ordinary 'tabs' event, so terminal and web code see only full lists; a 'tab' before any list, or for an unknown id, is ignored. Clients older than this protocol get the existing warning for an event they cannot read and recover on reload. Turn entry activates only the entering tab's trusted project plugin directories (projectContext.enter); full reconciliation against all open tabs (projectContext.refresh) runs on startup, navigation, close and trust changes.",
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		'Measured on the server (bun:jsc sampling profile of the live host, 319 open tabs, 11 clients): 76% of host samples were tabs.publish under host.broadcast; each publish rebuilt the 51 KB list (13 ms) and copied it per client (wire.copy about 4 ms each), about 80 ms, about 5 times per turn, capping hal -p batch classification near 2.5 calls/s.',
		'In-process bench (300 tabs, 11 clients, fake provider, 100 text chunks per turn; server CPU, process.cpuUsage, includes bench polling): before 342.6 ms CPU and 1910 KB to clients per turn; per-tab rows 50.3 ms and 7.0 KB; plus per-tab turn entry 35 ms. Turn-entry projectContext.refresh had been 39% of the remaining samples (realpath/exists per open tab). Next hotspots: notify.route, tabs file save per update, prompt-cache system prompt inspection, history-input.messages.',
		'Reviewed by openai/gpt-6-astra (session 53-yql): suggested coalescing broadcasts and bypassing the per-client wire copy as follow-ups; legacy-client negotiation was left out, since old clients already warn and reload.'
	],
	needs: []
}
